= Distribution shift =

== Overview ==
'''Distribution shift''' occurs when the data-generating distribution relevant at evaluation or deployment differs from the distribution represented by training data.

A model can generalize well within the training distribution and still fail after shift.

== Joint-distribution view ==
For inputs X and targets Y, training and target distributions can differ:

<pre>
P_train(X,Y) ≠ P_target(X,Y)
</pre>

Different assumptions about which parts change define different shift types.

== Covariate shift ==
Under covariate shift:

<pre>
P_train(X) ≠ P_target(X)
P_train(Y|X) = P_target(Y|X)
</pre>

Covariate shift is one special case, not the definition of all distribution shift.

== Other shifts ==
Related cases include label/prior shift, conditional/concept shift, domain shift, temporal drift, subgroup composition change, and changes in measurement processes.

Terminology varies across fields.

== Generalization ==
Ordinary iid test performance estimates performance under the sampled test distribution.

It does not by itself establish robustness to distribution shift.

== Causes ==
Shift can arise from:

* time;
* geography;
* policy changes;
* population changes;
* sensors or data pipelines;
* strategic behavior;
* intervention;
* deployment feedback;
* data-collection differences.

== Related concepts ==
[[Generalization (machine learning)]], [[Probability distribution]], [[Correlation]], [[Causality]], [[Spurious correlation]], and [[Shortcut learning]] help explain why shifted performance can fail.

